// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(5) lists exactly
// what shall not be declared `consteval`: "A destructor, allocation function,
// or deallocation function". An ordinary *non-static* member function is not
// in that list, so it may be declared `consteval`; §9.1(1)/(2) and
// front-matter §1(2) adopt [dcl.constexpr]/1-2 unchanged, and those permit it
// too.
// §9.1(4) then requires the call to produce a constant expression. The
// implicit object argument is part of the call, so the receiver must itself
// be usable in a constant expression -- here a named `constexpr` object,
// whose class satisfies docs/spec/en/06-constant-evaluation.md (ch06)
// §7.2(2) as required by §9.1(6).
// §7.2(6.1)/(6.2) require the implementation to support the `constexpr`
// constructor call and the `consteval` member call.
//
// Call position: block scope. Callee: `consteval` non-static member function.
// Receiver: named object.
//
// Expected value derived from the spec text above and independently confirmed
// with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which compiles this exact source and exits 42.
//
// CURRENT STATUS (scpp @ a59ffce): FAILS. scpp resolves the member call but
// then reports "error: internal error: no generated code for resolved
// function 'Counter_doubled' called here" -- the immediate member call is
// lowered as a runtime call instead of being folded. The expectation above
// comes from the spec and from clang; do not weaken it to match the
// implementation.
class Counter {
public:
    virtual ~Counter() = default;
public:
    int value{};

    constexpr Counter(int v) {
        this->value = v;
        return;
    }

    consteval int doubled() const {
        return this->value * 2;
    }
};

int main() {
    constexpr Counter c{21};
    return c.doubled();
}
